WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.760
- It's an epidemic that's coming to us.

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:06.290
It's this wave, a pulse of of the virus as

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:07.790
it's spreading from person to person

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:09.490
and growing exponentially.

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:12.660
And the reason that this
is very important is that,

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:14.420
let's say for the sake of argument

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:17.620
100,000 Americans are going
to die of this condition

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:19.270
in the next year.

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:21.040
It makes all the difference in the world,

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:23.140
whether those hundred thousand die over

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:24.810
the course of the next year,

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:27.800
or whether all 100,000
die in the next month.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:29.130
If they die in the next month,

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:31.800
they're gonna overwhelm
our healthcare system.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:35.540
And many more Americans will
die, that would have died

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:37.370
if we allowed our healthcare system

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:39.405
and our supply chain to work.

00:00:39.405 --> 00:00:41.988
(upbeat music)

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:51.120
- Joining me today is a
sociologist and physician

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.630
known for his research on social networks

00:00:53.630 --> 00:00:58.350
and the socio-economic,
bio-social and evolutionary

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:01.740
determinants of behavior,
health and longevity.

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:03.840
Nick Christakis, welcome
back to The Rubin Report.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:05.640
- Thank you for having me, Dave.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.360
- I'm glad to have you back,

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:08.810
wish we could be doing this live.

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:10.590
But obviously, we're practicing a little

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:12.593
social distancing right now.

00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:15.150
With everything going on with corona,

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:17.910
I've been trying to find a wide
array of people to talk to.

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:20.210
You put out, what I thought was a really

00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:22.620
fantastic Twitter thread.

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:23.940
I rarely send people to Twitter,

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:25.940
but you put out a great Twitter thread

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:28.170
that covered this through
all of the disciplines

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:30.130
that I just read there in your bio,

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:31.660
which are quite extensive.

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:33.660
So let's just start one at a time,

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.710
the two things that
people have heard the most

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:38.600
about at the moment, social distancing,

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:40.000
and flattening the curve.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.510
Can you just explain both

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:42.930
and why they're important right now?

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:46.340
- Yeah, so the virus takes
advantage of the fact that

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:48.010
we are social animals.

00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:50.930
That we assemble in groups,
that we have friends,

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:53.250
that we touch each other
and hug each other.

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:55.410
And it uses those connections

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:57.580
that we assemble ourselves
into these networks.

00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:00.000
It uses those connections as a highway

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.810
to travel through our species.

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:04.430
And so the challenge for us right now

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:06.380
is how to interrupt that highway,

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:08.960
how to break the paths that the virus uses

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:12.680
to travel from person to
person, to person to person.

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:15.920
The reason that's important
is that this flattening

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:19.030
the curve idea, so we're gonna
engage in social distancing,

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:21.480
we're gonna stop
interacting with each other,

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:24.210
we're gonna keep physically
apart from each other.

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:25.360
That's the most important thing.

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:29.060
In fact, I've been advocating
for a shift in terminology,

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:32.000
to physical distancing
from social distancing.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:34.810
In other words, we can
still interact socially,

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:38.302
but from a distance, like
you and I are doing now.

00:02:38.302 --> 00:02:40.840
And so we want physical distancing.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:42.550
It's irony we wanna like,

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:45.290
bond together, at the same
time as we are sort of

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:47.870
separating from each
other in terms physically.

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:49.890
Anyway, the reason we're engaging in that

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:52.380
physical distancing is to cut
the path through the network.

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:55.270
And the reason we're doing
that, is to flatten the curve.

00:02:55.270 --> 00:02:56.400
What does that mean?

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:58.900
Well, it's an epidemic
that's coming to us,

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:03.900
it's this wave, a pulse of
the virus as it's spreading

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:06.620
from person to person and
growing exponentially.

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:09.810
And the reason that this
is very important is that,

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:11.560
let's say, for the sake of argument,

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:14.760
100,000 Americans are going
to die of this condition

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:18.170
in the next year, it makes all
the difference in the world,

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:21.930
whether those 100,000 die over
the course of the next year,

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:24.930
or whether all 100,000
die in the next month.

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:26.270
If they die in the next month,

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:28.930
they're gonna overwhelm
our healthcare system,

00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:32.670
and many more Americans will
die than would have died

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:34.510
if we allowed our healthcare system

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:36.610
and our supply chain to work.

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:38.140
So we wanna flatten the curve.

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:41.650
We have got this peak
of a curve, the epidemic

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:43.040
is coming at us

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:45.230
and it's gonna hit lots of
people at the same time.

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:48.540
We wanna stop it from doing
that, and flatten the curve

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:51.060
and push it out into the future.

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:53.300
And when we do that, we
achieve a number of objectives.

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:56.330
First of all, at any given point,

00:03:56.330 --> 00:03:58.850
fewer people are sick
so our healthcare system

00:03:58.850 --> 00:04:00.190
can take care of them.

00:04:00.190 --> 00:04:02.720
Many more might live as a result of that.

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:07.720
Second, we push some of the
cases out into the future,

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:10.880
so that maybe some of the
people that gets sick eventually

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:14.200
will get sick at a time when
we've invented a vaccine,

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:16.550
or we know better how to treat the people.

00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:19.780
So we also gain that benefit
by flattening the curve.

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:23.030
And as I said, not only do
we decompress our healthcare

00:04:23.030 --> 00:04:25.360
system, not only do we push
people into the future,

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:27.390
when we might be better
able to care for them,

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:29.620
but we reduce mortality as well.

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:33.180
So maybe instead of 100,000
dying, maybe 90,000 die

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:34.480
if we push it forward.

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:36.430
So that's why we're social distancing

00:04:36.430 --> 00:04:38.530
and that's what flattening
the curve means.

00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:40.440
- So I guess the obvious
follow up then is,

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:43.220
do we have any evidence that
the things that we are doing

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:46.000
at the moment or being
told to do is working?

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:49.281
Is the curve being flattened?

00:04:49.281 --> 00:04:51.330
- We cannot know yet because,

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:54.280
we have not had adequate
testing in this country.

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:56.340
We don't have adequate surveillance,

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:58.750
like they did in China and Korea.

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:00.830
And so we really can't be sure.

00:05:00.830 --> 00:05:03.740
We know this type of behavior,

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:05.050
this social distancing behavior,

00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:08.810
the banning of public gatherings,
the closure of schools,

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:12.810
the reduction in group
size, the self-isolation,

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:16.070
we know that that works
from other epidemics,

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:18.440
but we cannot yet know
how much it's working in

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:20.130
the United States right now

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:21.990
because we don't have
the kind of surveillance

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:23.400
we need to see it.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:25.700
- So I think when people
hear about social distancing,

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:27.600
and as you called it physical distancing,

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:29.720
I think people are confused or worried.

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:31.360
What about the people that are actually

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:35.007
in the house with them
family, kids, parents,

00:05:35.007 --> 00:05:37.140
whatever it is, that might have to go out

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:38.944
and then come back into the house?

00:05:38.944 --> 00:05:43.944
- Yeah, that's a risk that we
run and that the Chinese ran,

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:48.410
when they locked down 930
million people in their countries

00:05:48.410 --> 00:05:50.310
since January the 25th.

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:53.150
So typically, what you do is you try

00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:55.280
to compress all of your shopping,

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:57.800
in fact Americans should begin
to be doing that right now.

00:05:57.800 --> 00:05:59.860
Instead of going out three
times a week to buy stuff

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:02.300
go out once a week, compress everything,

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:04.820
minimize the amount of
time you're out and about.

00:06:04.820 --> 00:06:07.600
When the person does go
out, they should ideally

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:09.440
try to wash their hands frequently,

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:11.520
not press elevator buttons.

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:14.010
The Chinese have all these
ingenious things where,

00:06:14.010 --> 00:06:15.000
when you go into an elevator,

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:18.370
there's like a little pin
cushion of toothpicks,

00:06:18.370 --> 00:06:20.380
and you take a toothpick,
you press your button,

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:22.190
and then you discard the toothpick.

00:06:22.190 --> 00:06:24.090
So that you're not
touching buttons because

00:06:24.090 --> 00:06:26.880
the virus can spread on smooth surfaces,

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:28.150
that's called fomites.

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:31.540
So, a person leaving the
household should do that.

00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:33.570
If someone in the household does get sick

00:06:33.570 --> 00:06:36.380
and they are tested positive,
you can, in principle,

00:06:36.380 --> 00:06:38.090
isolate that person for two weeks

00:06:38.090 --> 00:06:39.890
from other members of the household,

00:06:39.890 --> 00:06:41.070
or in the worst case scenario,

00:06:41.070 --> 00:06:43.330
they infect the other
people in the household,

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:46.510
but nevertheless, it's then
constrained to one household.

00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:48.490
Keep in mind that, most people infected by

00:06:48.490 --> 00:06:50.083
this pathogen do fine.

00:06:52.850 --> 00:06:55.340
The great majority of
people will have no symptoms

00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:57.150
or will have minor symptoms.

00:06:57.150 --> 00:07:02.150
Some people however, maybe
25% over will get sick,

00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:04.270
and of those, maybe a fifth of those

00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:07.450
so total of 5% maybe
of the people infected,

00:07:07.450 --> 00:07:09.410
will get seriously ill.

00:07:09.410 --> 00:07:12.140
And then of course, on
average, we don't know exactly

00:07:12.140 --> 00:07:15.780
how many of the people
who are infected will die.

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:17.100
And the reason, again we don't know is

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:18.180
we don't have good testing.

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:21.120
So we don't know what the denominator is,

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:24.810
we don't know of 10,000
people who are infected,

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:26.260
we know that because they were tested,

00:07:26.260 --> 00:07:28.620
let's say, what happens to them?

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:30.120
And how many of them get sick?

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:32.262
And how many of them need ICU care?

00:07:32.262 --> 00:07:33.350
And how many of them die?

00:07:33.350 --> 00:07:35.610
We don't know that denominator accurately.

00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:38.340
But our best estimate is
that, of those infected

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:39.980
somewhere between half a percent

00:07:39.980 --> 00:07:42.300
and one and a half percent will die.

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:47.300
And that's a large number,
but it's very graded by age.

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:52.710
So if you're younger than 25,
your risk of death is very

00:07:52.710 --> 00:07:56.980
small, maybe 0.2% that's a
big risk for young person,

00:07:56.980 --> 00:07:58.990
but nevertheless, it's a small risk.

00:07:58.990 --> 00:08:02.120
And then middle-aged
people around 50 or 60,

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:05.970
it's about 1%, and then
above 80, it's about 20%.

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:08.090
So it's a very steep curve with age.

00:08:08.090 --> 00:08:09.360
- Do you have a recommendation then

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:11.310
if you're in the household right now,

00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:13.500
and you're any of these
ages, let's say you're 25,

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:16.430
you're 50, and you're 80,
let's just go with those ages.

00:08:16.430 --> 00:08:18.370
And you're not feeling great,

00:08:18.370 --> 00:08:20.670
should you be then trying
to go to the hospital

00:08:20.670 --> 00:08:23.830
or I guess maybe if you're
80 you are, or just stay home

00:08:23.830 --> 00:08:25.250
and deal with it the best you can.

00:08:25.250 --> 00:08:28.300
- Yeah, in general, one
of the things we all need

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:30.530
to be very good about doing right now is,

00:08:30.530 --> 00:08:32.680
giving our healthcare workers a break.

00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:34.790
So you should not be going to the hospital

00:08:34.790 --> 00:08:36.430
with a sniffles right now.

00:08:36.430 --> 00:08:37.860
There are a few things
that Americans should do,

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:39.150
you should get a flu shot.

00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:40.730
One of the reasons you
should get a flu shot

00:08:40.730 --> 00:08:44.170
is not because the flu shot prevents you

00:08:44.170 --> 00:08:46.790
from getting coronavirus, it does not.

00:08:46.790 --> 00:08:47.870
But if you get a flu shot,

00:08:47.870 --> 00:08:50.700
it lowers your risk of
actually getting the flu

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:54.400
and therefore, you won't go
to the hospital with the flu.

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:55.910
And now is not a good time for you,

00:08:55.910 --> 00:08:58.070
to be burdening our healthcare
system for your own sake,

00:08:58.070 --> 00:09:00.800
or for the sake of the system.

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:02.020
Furthermore, going to the hospital

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:05.650
if you're not actually sick,
or if you're just mildly sick,

00:09:05.650 --> 00:09:08.130
there are a lot of actually
seriously sick people there,

00:09:08.130 --> 00:09:10.588
you might get the germ from them.

00:09:10.588 --> 00:09:14.020
In a hospital waiting room
is not a time to be right now

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:15.710
unless you actually need it.

00:09:15.710 --> 00:09:18.830
So generally speaking, the
symptoms of coronavirus

00:09:18.830 --> 00:09:22.310
are variable, you should
have a fever above 101.5,

00:09:22.310 --> 00:09:23.900
it's in about 85% of people,

00:09:23.900 --> 00:09:25.910
not everyone but most people have a fever.

00:09:25.910 --> 00:09:28.510
A dry cough would be a symptom

00:09:28.510 --> 00:09:30.730
and then there's a shortness of breath.

00:09:30.730 --> 00:09:32.620
Certainly, if you have
shortness of breath and a fever,

00:09:32.620 --> 00:09:34.720
absolutely, you should go to the hospital.

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:38.020
And then there's a whole
host of other symptoms

00:09:38.020 --> 00:09:39.770
at lower percentages.

00:09:39.770 --> 00:09:44.770
So if you're a 25-year-old
person and you have a runny nose,

00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:47.240
and low great temperature
and some muscle aches,

00:09:47.240 --> 00:09:48.780
it's probably not coronavirus,

00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:51.020
stay home until you get sicker.

00:09:51.020 --> 00:09:53.250
That would be a reasonable choice.

00:09:53.250 --> 00:09:54.420
If you're an older person,

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:56.440
then you should have a lower threshold.

00:09:56.440 --> 00:09:59.773
And ideally, if you
went, we would test you,

00:10:01.430 --> 00:10:03.190
but we can't, we don't
have enough testing yet.

00:10:03.190 --> 00:10:05.160
We're hopefully the country
will get better at that

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:06.210
in the coming months.

00:10:06.210 --> 00:10:07.890
- Yeah, all right, I wanna
keep going through this

00:10:07.890 --> 00:10:10.400
Twitter thread 'cause
you hit it just sort of

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:12.600
at every possible level.

00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:14.850
So you talked about
reasons to close schools,

00:10:14.850 --> 00:10:17.160
even if kids there aren't sick.

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:19.060
- Yeah, I mean, school closures,

00:10:19.060 --> 00:10:19.893
there's a lot of debate about this.

00:10:19.893 --> 00:10:22.340
There are two kinds of school closures,

00:10:22.340 --> 00:10:24.400
and most of the country's
shutting the schools anyway now.

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:26.100
So this point is, is behind us.

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:28.370
I was sending out this
information like three

00:10:28.370 --> 00:10:31.100
or four weeks ago, but school
closures are two kinds.

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:32.600
There's reactive school closure,

00:10:32.600 --> 00:10:35.440
when there's a case in the
school, and you close the school

00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:36.920
and that's pretty uncontroversial.

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:38.813
I mean, most parents, teachers,
public health experts,

00:10:38.813 --> 00:10:41.500
there's an outbreak of
epidemic in the school.

00:10:41.500 --> 00:10:43.520
Let's close the school.

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:45.520
And it's been shown that if you do that,

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:48.810
if you close the schools in an area,

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:52.280
you lower the cumulative
prevalence of the condition

00:10:52.280 --> 00:10:56.700
by about 25% and postpone the
epidemic by a week or two,

00:10:56.700 --> 00:10:57.670
which is good.

00:10:57.670 --> 00:11:02.310
So, reactive school closures
helpful but, it's not as good

00:11:02.310 --> 00:11:04.120
as we can do that even better,

00:11:04.120 --> 00:11:06.220
is something called
proactive school closure.

00:11:06.220 --> 00:11:07.960
When you close the schools,

00:11:07.960 --> 00:11:09.810
even before there's a case in the schools.

00:11:09.810 --> 00:11:12.740
For instance, you living
in a mid-size town,

00:11:12.740 --> 00:11:15.620
there's a case in the
community, it's something called

00:11:15.620 --> 00:11:17.580
the community-acquired case.

00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:20.110
So it's not like someone
from Italy or China

