00:26:26.160 --> 00:26:28.290
or three audience questions real quick.

00:26:28.290 --> 00:26:32.740
You mentioned the 1957
flu pandemic before.

00:26:32.740 --> 00:26:35.550
Can you talk about some of the
similarities and differences

00:26:35.550 --> 00:26:36.383
to what we're dealing with now?

00:26:36.383 --> 00:26:41.180
- Yeah, well 1918, of
course, was the big one.

00:26:41.180 --> 00:26:45.623
That was the so called Spanish
Flu, which swept the world,

00:26:46.530 --> 00:26:49.160
killed many millions of
people, including our country.

00:26:49.160 --> 00:26:50.290
It was a different time.

00:26:50.290 --> 00:26:53.600
Of course, people were starving

00:26:53.600 --> 00:26:56.270
because of the war, there
was a lot of disruption

00:26:56.270 --> 00:26:57.423
because of the war.

00:26:58.820 --> 00:27:00.923
And it was a different pathogen,

00:27:02.710 --> 00:27:05.480
and there was no modern
medicine, no antibiotics,

00:27:05.480 --> 00:27:09.520
even against bacteria, and
we think many of those people

00:27:09.520 --> 00:27:12.290
died, not just because
of the viral infection,

00:27:12.290 --> 00:27:15.150
but then their lungs got
so called super infected

00:27:15.150 --> 00:27:19.010
with bacterial pneumonia,
which killed them, many people.

00:27:19.010 --> 00:27:22.070
So a lot was different then,
and we earlier we talked about

00:27:22.070 --> 00:27:25.623
how they came in three
waves and it was awful.

00:27:26.470 --> 00:27:27.990
And there was a great
depression of course,

00:27:27.990 --> 00:27:29.340
afterwards as well.

00:27:29.340 --> 00:27:33.970
So, I don't think, around the same time.

00:27:33.970 --> 00:27:38.970
Anyway, I don't think that
this pandemic is thankfully

00:27:39.930 --> 00:27:41.020
gonna be that bad.

00:27:41.020 --> 00:27:43.570
But the nearest analog I can see,

00:27:43.570 --> 00:27:45.200
even though it was a different pathogen,

00:27:45.200 --> 00:27:47.660
and in some ways had
different epidemiology.

00:27:47.660 --> 00:27:51.350
For example, in 1957, the
pathogen also killed young people,

00:27:51.350 --> 00:27:52.650
whereas this one does not.

00:27:54.020 --> 00:27:56.870
I think '57 might be a good
model for what we're facing.

00:27:56.870 --> 00:28:00.500
So every 10 years or so, at risk viably,

00:28:00.500 --> 00:28:03.960
but at an unpredictable time,
the world has a pandemic.

00:28:03.960 --> 00:28:08.620
Most of your listeners will
remember SARS, or H1N1, or MERS

00:28:08.620 --> 00:28:09.840
and those have petered out,

00:28:09.840 --> 00:28:12.550
so people they don't take them seriously

00:28:12.550 --> 00:28:15.100
and they peter out for
a variety of reasons.

00:28:15.100 --> 00:28:17.020
Ironically, one of the reasons is that,

00:28:17.020 --> 00:28:18.230
sometimes they're too deadly.

00:28:18.230 --> 00:28:21.640
For instance, SARS was
about 10 times as deadly,

00:28:21.640 --> 00:28:24.990
as the COVID that we're facing now.

00:28:24.990 --> 00:28:26.610
And when a disease is too deadly,

00:28:26.610 --> 00:28:29.980
it kills its victim too fast,
so the person can't spread it.

00:28:29.980 --> 00:28:33.260
So this particular pathogen we're facing,

00:28:33.260 --> 00:28:35.760
has sort of middling level lethality,

00:28:35.760 --> 00:28:38.500
it's neither too benign, nor too deadly,

00:28:38.500 --> 00:28:40.730
it's not as deadly as the 1918 virus,

00:28:40.730 --> 00:28:43.500
and it has middling
level transmissibility.

00:28:43.500 --> 00:28:45.700
It's not very difficult to transmit,

00:28:45.700 --> 00:28:47.920
and it's not too hard to transmit.

00:28:47.920 --> 00:28:50.850
And it's sort of in the
middle there and in my view,

00:28:50.850 --> 00:28:54.370
it seems to be behaving
similar to the '57 pandemic,

00:28:54.370 --> 00:28:56.100
which should be clear.

00:28:56.100 --> 00:28:58.980
Some of your listeners
older ones may remember it,

00:28:58.980 --> 00:29:00.430
but many people won't.

00:29:00.430 --> 00:29:02.360
And part of the reason
people don't remember this,

00:29:02.360 --> 00:29:05.300
we didn't have the same
media environment then

00:29:05.300 --> 00:29:07.560
and of course, people
were dealing with polio

00:29:07.560 --> 00:29:09.280
and other deadly diseases.

00:29:09.280 --> 00:29:11.640
But that pandemic swept
over the United States

00:29:11.640 --> 00:29:14.770
and killed we think about a
hundred and 10,000 people,

00:29:14.770 --> 00:29:18.290
which back then, was half as
deadly as all of the cancer

00:29:18.290 --> 00:29:20.010
in the United States.

00:29:20.010 --> 00:29:23.510
So today, if we have a '57 pandemic,

00:29:23.510 --> 00:29:26.350
we're talking hundreds of
thousands of Americans dying

00:29:26.350 --> 00:29:30.363
from COVID, if the disease
is half as deadly as cancer,

00:29:31.230 --> 00:29:32.320
as a group.

00:29:32.320 --> 00:29:34.440
So it's serious, it
could be quite serious.

00:29:34.440 --> 00:29:37.660
I also wanna just take an
opportunity again to say,

00:29:37.660 --> 00:29:39.580
we don't know exactly what's gonna happen

00:29:39.580 --> 00:29:41.540
and what's very important
for your listeners is

00:29:41.540 --> 00:29:45.793
to understand that despite
political polarization,

00:29:47.450 --> 00:29:50.760
and despite our desire
to simplify our choices,

00:29:50.760 --> 00:29:55.090
should we have rice krispies,
or should we have cornflakes?

00:29:55.090 --> 00:29:58.110
There are shades of gray and
there's nuance when it comes

00:29:58.110 --> 00:30:00.690
to serious policy dilemmas,

00:30:00.690 --> 00:30:03.190
and serious scientific questions.

00:30:03.190 --> 00:30:05.950
So there's a range of
outcomes we could observe

00:30:05.950 --> 00:30:07.190
in the United States from this.

00:30:07.190 --> 00:30:10.500
We don't know exactly
what's going to happen,

00:30:10.500 --> 00:30:14.710
but at best, 35,000
Americans are going to die,

00:30:14.710 --> 00:30:16.410
in my view, at best.

00:30:16.410 --> 00:30:19.890
And it's quite possible
that 20% of Americans

00:30:19.890 --> 00:30:21.180
will get infected.

00:30:21.180 --> 00:30:23.711
That's 60 million
Americans in the next year.

00:30:23.711 --> 00:30:26.080
And our best estimate
is that half a percent

00:30:26.080 --> 00:30:28.003
of those people might die.

00:30:29.180 --> 00:30:32.430
That's 300,000 Americans dying

00:30:32.430 --> 00:30:35.410
in the next year of this condition.

00:30:35.410 --> 00:30:36.980
And that's possible.

00:30:36.980 --> 00:30:38.510
We're not sure it's gonna happen.

00:30:38.510 --> 00:30:39.350
It might not happen.

00:30:39.350 --> 00:30:40.740
I hope it doesn't happen,

00:30:40.740 --> 00:30:44.773
but it's possible and that
is a very, very heavy burden.

00:30:46.290 --> 00:30:48.660
That's 1957 pandemic.

00:30:48.660 --> 00:30:51.440
That's half as deadly as
cancer, approximately.

00:30:51.440 --> 00:30:54.120
So that's why everyone is taken seriously.

