and maybe you can get to some of them.

00:33:36.500 --> 00:33:38.264
But there were also a bunch about this.

00:33:38.264 --> 00:33:40.069
Is there any confirmation whether

00:33:40.069 --> 00:33:42.160
airborne transmission is possible?

00:33:42.160 --> 00:33:44.429
I mean, if someone has it
and you're at the store,

00:33:44.429 --> 00:33:46.607
and you're avoiding
everybody the best you can

00:33:46.607 --> 00:33:48.500
and you're wearing gloves
and the rest of it,

00:33:48.500 --> 00:33:50.680
but somebody breathes on
you or sneezes on you,

00:33:50.680 --> 00:33:53.299
or something like that. I think
there's a little confusion

00:33:53.299 --> 00:33:56.729
as to what airborne transmission
actually mean maybe.

00:33:56.729 --> 00:33:59.090
- Yeah, so droplet transmission

00:33:59.090 --> 00:34:03.557
is when a liquid, like I
sneeze or cough or spit,

00:34:03.557 --> 00:34:05.790
and they're droplets
that go through the air

00:34:05.790 --> 00:34:07.543
and land on you, in
your nose or your mouth

00:34:07.543 --> 00:34:09.300
and transmits it to you,

00:34:09.300 --> 00:34:10.360
and that's why we're supposed to stay,

00:34:10.360 --> 00:34:12.681
four or five or six feet
away from each other

00:34:12.681 --> 00:34:15.191
because if I sneeze and
I'm six feet away from you,

00:34:15.191 --> 00:34:17.110
the droplets would go through the air

00:34:17.110 --> 00:34:19.690
and then fall into the
ground and not reach you.

00:34:19.690 --> 00:34:22.591
Airborne is when tiny particulates,
maybe the virus itself

00:34:22.591 --> 00:34:26.236
are floating through the
air, and can reach you.

00:34:26.236 --> 00:34:28.960
We believe that there is both droplet

00:34:28.960 --> 00:34:30.580
and airborne transmission,

00:34:30.580 --> 00:34:33.710
and is probably fecal transmission as well

00:34:33.710 --> 00:34:36.630
which is a little bit
concerning of this pathogen

00:34:36.630 --> 00:34:39.190
and something called fomite transmission,

00:34:39.190 --> 00:34:42.507
which is that the pathogen
can stay on surfaces,

00:34:42.507 --> 00:34:44.330
especially smooth surfaces.

00:34:44.330 --> 00:34:49.330
So porous things like paper
money or fabrics, less so,

00:34:49.350 --> 00:34:52.650
but smooth surfaces like
elevator buttons, doorknobs,

00:34:52.650 --> 00:34:56.270
kitchen counters, can stay more.

00:34:56.270 --> 00:34:58.151
But the virus is also very fragile.

00:34:58.151 --> 00:35:00.623
It's encapsulated in a lipid Kote.

00:35:02.490 --> 00:35:04.859
The viruses work by
taking over our own cells,

00:35:04.859 --> 00:35:06.500
and they grow inside ourselves,

00:35:06.500 --> 00:35:08.180
and then they explode out of ourselves.

00:35:08.180 --> 00:35:09.550
And as they explode out,

00:35:09.550 --> 00:35:11.233
they take a little bit of
our cell membrane with them,

00:35:11.233 --> 00:35:14.483
so they're encapsulated
in this lipid membrane,

00:35:15.350 --> 00:35:18.217
and that helps them to
be more transmissible,

00:35:18.217 --> 00:35:21.400
but it makes them very vulnerable to soap.

00:35:21.400 --> 00:35:23.819
So cleanliness, this is
why washing your hands,

00:35:23.819 --> 00:35:25.300
and when you wash your hands,

00:35:25.300 --> 00:35:26.738
you should wash between your fingers

00:35:26.738 --> 00:35:28.730
and the backs of your hands.

00:35:28.730 --> 00:35:30.824
Many people are saying,
"Sing the Happy Birthday song

00:35:30.824 --> 00:35:32.990
"twice while you wash your hands."

00:35:32.990 --> 00:35:35.230
It's true, but that does kill the virus.

00:35:35.230 --> 00:35:36.960
So when you go out and about,

00:35:36.960 --> 00:35:38.310
if you avoid touching your face,

00:35:38.310 --> 00:35:40.470
if you wash your hands
before you return home,

00:35:40.470 --> 00:35:41.745
that is very basic hygiene.

00:35:41.745 --> 00:35:43.680
That's actually very helpful.

00:35:43.680 --> 00:35:45.360
And experiments have
shown this by the way,

00:35:45.360 --> 00:35:46.866
it's not just old wives tales.

00:35:46.866 --> 00:35:50.150
Like people actually do
studies where they touch stuff,

00:35:50.150 --> 00:35:51.180
and then they wash their hands

00:35:51.180 --> 00:35:52.679
and they see if the virus goes away.

00:35:52.679 --> 00:35:53.710
- Yeah, all right.

00:35:53.710 --> 00:35:56.700
So the last one then is I got
a couple of versions of this

00:35:56.700 --> 00:35:58.462
that we're all concerned about this

00:35:58.462 --> 00:36:00.979
and even the way I'm
trying to do these shows,

00:36:00.979 --> 00:36:03.051
I'm not trying to
overload people too much.

00:36:03.051 --> 00:36:06.130
And I don't want everyone
glued to their TVs endlessly.

00:36:06.130 --> 00:36:08.030
What else do you make of just sort of

00:36:08.030 --> 00:36:12.498
the human condition in the
midst of a pandemic like this?

00:36:12.498 --> 00:36:14.284
- Well, as you know,
because we talked about it

00:36:14.284 --> 00:36:17.000
the last time you and I
met, I just wrote this book.

00:36:17.000 --> 00:36:18.170
- Yeah, you got a whole book about it.

00:36:18.170 --> 00:36:19.730
It's right over there.

00:36:19.730 --> 00:36:20.710
- I know.

00:36:20.710 --> 00:36:21.543
I wrote this book called,

00:36:21.543 --> 00:36:24.120
"Blueprint: The Evolutionary
Origins of a Good Society."

00:36:24.120 --> 00:36:26.750
And as you know, Dave, I'm an optimist.

00:36:26.750 --> 00:36:28.460
I believe in human beings.

00:36:28.460 --> 00:36:31.652
I marvel at our capacity for goodness.

00:36:31.652 --> 00:36:35.320
I recognize our capacity
for evil and selfishness

00:36:35.320 --> 00:36:38.710
and tribalism and violence.

00:36:38.710 --> 00:36:41.730
But I also revere and
admire our capacity for love

00:36:41.730 --> 00:36:44.700
and friendship, and
cooperation and teaching.

00:36:44.700 --> 00:36:47.340
In fact, all of these are things
that we're using right now

00:36:47.340 --> 00:36:48.731
to confront this virus.

00:36:48.731 --> 00:36:51.070
We are banding together
and working together

00:36:51.070 --> 00:36:52.890
to fight off this invader.

00:36:52.890 --> 00:36:54.950
We are using our capacity for teaching.

00:36:54.950 --> 00:36:57.990
The reason we know what
to do in this case,

00:36:57.990 --> 00:37:00.864
is because previous humans
who confronted this virus

00:37:00.864 --> 00:37:03.427
wrote down, "Here's what you do

00:37:03.427 --> 00:37:05.300
"when you're facing a pandemic."

00:37:05.300 --> 00:37:06.210
You literally can go...

00:37:06.210 --> 00:37:07.527
There's a book right here, I can look over

00:37:07.527 --> 00:37:09.027
and see it's called,

00:37:09.027 --> 00:37:11.960
"National Strategy for
Influenza Pandemics."

00:37:11.960 --> 00:37:14.993
Like there are books that you
can open up like a cookbook

00:37:14.993 --> 00:37:18.030
and they say, "Here's what you
do when there's a pandemic."

00:37:18.030 --> 00:37:19.100
It's amazing.

00:37:19.100 --> 00:37:20.824
We can transmit this
knowledge to each other

00:37:20.824 --> 00:37:24.270
and therefore, cope with this enemy,

00:37:24.270 --> 00:37:26.917
the virus that is attacking us.

00:37:26.917 --> 00:37:29.750
So this is why I am
hopeful and optimistic.

00:37:29.750 --> 00:37:33.515
The virus will eventually
pass, our species will survive.

00:37:33.515 --> 00:37:36.220
It is unfortunate that many will die.

00:37:36.220 --> 00:37:37.760
We don't know how many.

00:37:37.760 --> 00:37:39.299
The virus will become endemic.

00:37:39.299 --> 00:37:41.720
We will see the other side of this,

00:37:41.720 --> 00:37:44.279
but we do have to work
together as a society

00:37:44.279 --> 00:37:46.570
to shore up our industries,

00:37:46.570 --> 00:37:48.960
to protect our healthcare workers,

00:37:48.960 --> 00:37:51.560
from whom we're gonna
ask tremendous amounts,

00:37:51.560 --> 00:37:54.310
and from whom we're gonna
ask to take tremendous risks.

00:37:55.270 --> 00:37:57.196
When I was a doctor, I
mean, I'm a physician

00:37:57.196 --> 00:37:58.830
and I don't see patients anymore,

00:37:58.830 --> 00:38:01.800
but when I was patients,
especially in the 1990s,

00:38:01.800 --> 00:38:04.270
the HIV epidemic was very prominent.

00:38:04.270 --> 00:38:05.429
And there was a lot of concern

00:38:05.429 --> 00:38:08.680
that if you took care
of patients with HIV,

00:38:08.680 --> 00:38:10.340
and a lot of those patients were gay,

00:38:10.340 --> 00:38:12.570
or they were from Haiti,
or they were drug users,

00:38:12.570 --> 00:38:15.680
they were groups that back then
were especially stigmatized.

00:38:15.680 --> 00:38:16.927
And so there was a lot of like, kind of,

00:38:16.927 --> 00:38:20.658
"Oh, well, we won't take
care of those people."

00:38:20.658 --> 00:38:23.120
And there was fear among
healthcare providers,

00:38:23.120 --> 00:38:25.950
like if you stuck them, and
you got their blood on you,

00:38:25.950 --> 00:38:28.050
you might contract this deadly disease.

00:38:28.050 --> 00:38:30.543
But we were expected
to take personal risks.

00:38:30.543 --> 00:38:32.680
We were expected to
care for these patients.

00:38:32.680 --> 00:38:34.706
It's a calling to be a doctor or a nurse.

00:38:34.706 --> 00:38:37.359
It's a moral obligation.

00:38:37.359 --> 00:38:40.649
And so we expect a huge amount
from our doctors and nurses

00:38:40.649 --> 00:38:42.830
and health care providers right now.

00:38:42.830 --> 00:38:44.500
We expect them to take these risks.

00:38:44.500 --> 00:38:48.410
But it's not the same to
expect them to take the risks

00:38:48.410 --> 00:38:49.940
with the proper equipment

00:38:50.820 --> 00:38:53.130
versus to send them to certain death.

00:38:53.130 --> 00:38:57.070
That is to say without masks,
gloves, protective equipment.

00:38:57.070 --> 00:38:58.206
And so I'm very concerned

00:38:58.206 --> 00:39:00.670
that we're not taking this serious,

00:39:00.670 --> 00:39:02.420
we don't have the right equipment

00:39:02.420 --> 00:39:05.347
to equip our frontline medical
personnel to deal with this.

00:39:05.347 --> 00:39:08.360
And this is why I'm also
upset that we squandered

00:39:08.360 --> 00:39:10.970
the six weeks that the
Chinese bought for us

00:39:10.970 --> 00:39:12.940
by locking down their nation.

00:39:12.940 --> 00:39:16.187
We should have been planning
to equip our hospitals

00:39:16.187 --> 00:39:19.561
with this personal protective
equipment, with ventilators,

00:39:19.561 --> 00:39:23.530
with staffing, with procedures
at which we can discuss,

00:39:23.530 --> 00:39:27.190
it's too technical and boring,
to confront this epidemic.

00:39:27.190 --> 00:39:30.271
So we're behind now, is the problem,

00:39:30.271 --> 00:39:32.077
and I hope very much we can catch up.

00:39:32.077 --> 00:39:35.033
And this is again, why social
distancing is so important

00:39:35.033 --> 00:39:38.940
to slow this thing down so
we can let our supply chain,

00:39:38.940 --> 00:39:42.532
our scientists, our politicians, frankly,

00:39:42.532 --> 00:39:45.730
catch up so that we can cope with this.

00:39:45.730 --> 00:39:48.498
- Well, Nick, I always consider
you one of the cleanest,

00:39:48.498 --> 00:39:50.800
clearest thinkers that we've got,

00:39:50.800 --> 00:39:51.980
and especially in a time like this,

00:39:51.980 --> 00:39:53.747
so I appreciate you taking the time.

00:39:53.747 --> 00:39:54.770
And maybe we'll get you back on

00:39:54.770 --> 00:39:56.809
in a couple weeks as
this thing continues to

00:39:56.809 --> 00:39:58.351
(overlapping conversation)
- Yes.

00:39:58.351 --> 00:40:00.470
Thank you very much for having, David,

00:40:00.470 --> 00:40:01.520
and thank you to your listeners.

00:40:01.520 --> 00:40:02.370
- Hey everyone, we're obviously

00:40:02.370 --> 00:40:04.460
in some unchartered
territory with coronavirus,

00:40:04.460 --> 00:40:06.540
and our plan here is to
help you make as much sense

00:40:06.540 --> 00:40:09.310
of the situation in a
nonalarmist fashion as possible.

00:40:09.310 --> 00:40:11.920
If you're looking for reliable
information from experts

00:40:11.920 --> 00:40:13.940
on the frontlines of the pandemic,

00:40:13.940 --> 00:40:15.396
check out our coronavirus playlist,

00:40:15.396 --> 00:40:17.937
which we'll keep adding
updates to right over here.

00:40:17.937 --> 00:40:20.430
And to get notified of all future videos

00:40:20.430 --> 00:40:21.480
be sure to subscribe.

